<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johan Maurits of Nassau-Siegen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1604">1604</a>–<a href="/years/1679">1679</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as John Maurice of Nassau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johan Maurits of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> count, later prince, who governed Dutch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> from 1637 to 1644. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritsstad">Mauritsstad</a> beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a>, restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> production, tolerated Catholics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and seized Portuguese slave-trading posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. He brought artists and scientists to record Brazil. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> he built the Mauritshuis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-maurits-of-nassau-siegen.jpg" alt="Johan Maurits of Nassau-Siegen" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan de Baen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Johan_Maurits_(1604-1679),_Count_of_Nassau-Siegen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
